<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Shifu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Shifu was a Chinese playwright of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, author of The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-western-chamber">Romance of the Western Chamber</a>, a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaju">zaju</a> drama of love between the scholar Zhang Sheng and Cui Yingying. Based on a Tang story, the play was one of the most popular works of Chinese literature for centuries and was often criticised by moralists for its celebration of romantic love.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wang Shifu, whose personal name was Wang Dexin, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>; his family's ancestral home was at Baoding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>. He came of a noble family, and his father was a general in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. Following the example of his elders, Wang began an official career as a county official and was later promoted to investigating censor of Shanxi Province for his talent and achievements. His character and disposition, however, led him to leave official life in the end and devote himself to writing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Fourteen plays are attributed to Wang, of which only three survive: The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-western-chamber">Romance of the Western Chamber</a> (Xi Xiang Ji), Li Chun Hall and Po Yao Ji. He is counted a representative of the Wencaipai, the literary talent group, in the literature of Chinese opera.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-western-chamber">Romance of the Western Chamber</a> is an amplified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaju">zaju</a>, the theatrical form popular in his day. Set against the orthodox repertoire of Yuan zaju, Wang developed a new format of his own. The play dramatises and reworks a love story already popular in the 12th century, and shares its plot with other zaju.</p><p>Wang's way of writing is marked by a delicate, indirect portrayal of his characters' changing feelings, through which he shapes them, and by foreshadowing, which gives his plots unexpected turns and lets him show the complexity of life. Because the play achieved popular success with a form of his own invention, it has been suggested that Wang may have been an experienced professional of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in the first age of Chinese drama.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wang Shifu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They are major contributions to Chinese literary culture, with playwrights such as Guan Hanqing and <b>Wang Shifu</b>, author of The Romance of the Western Chamber.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Khanbaliq, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.93,116.4&amp;year=1329" aria-label="Khanbaliq on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7417 735 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wang Shifu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7867 1005h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7867 1005h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.93,116.4&amp;year=1329">What was here in 1329? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
